 off of a farm and  just taking away the phosphorus  fertilizers  we added to help the crop and so here's  a little animation of a farmer  spraying their crops with a bunch of  phosphorus because again it's one of the  ingredients of fertilizers and so now  that phosphorus has been added to the  crops  the problem is now that phosphorus has  been added to the crops next time it  rains the rain water will simply carry  the phosphorus downhill and eventually  accumulates in this little pond that you  see in my animation  well this often leads to an explosion of  algae this is causes what are called  algal blooms remember phosphorous is a  fertilizer  it helps plant and plant like organisms  grow and so the algae responds to the  extra amounts of phosphorous by growing  causes what I called algal blooms here's  a picture from overhead of an algal  bloom you can see the green layer of  algae in this little watery environment  here and again another picture you can  see how green the water looks so why is  this bad well this leads to a problem  called eutrophication and this actually  causes a dead zone so this water which  is now full of algae ironically life  begins to suffocate you would think why  is it suffocating aren't the algae  photosynthetic and putting oxygen into  the water  well that's true at first but over time  this causes a growth of bacteria and  over time when the bacteria die in decay  that decaying process removes a lot of  the oxygen from the water so organisms  like fish literally suffocate and die it  causes a dead zone that's the process  called eutrophication so sadly again  because of human contributions the  phosphorous cycle is out of balance and  we hope that we can be responsible with  how we use fertilizers and so perhaps  look for natural fertilizers instead of  store-bought fertilizers and you know  you can also support organic farming in  farmers markets because these are foods  that are typically grown without  fertilizers and also pesticides moving  on to the next cycle so the final cycle  I want to mention is called the nitrogen  cycle well you know there's a problem  when it comes to our ability to use  night  most of the nitrogen that's in the  atmosphere by the way 78% of our  atmosphere is end to nitrogen gas the  problem is it's not usable and here's a  reason why the molecule n2 the nitrogen  that's in the atmosphere it's held  together by a very stable triple bond  and because it's held together by a very  stable triple bond our cells really  aren't able to convert it and break it  down so there's a problem we have to get  nitrogen but the nitrogen in the air is  not usable so we'll go over where the  nitrogen then comes from well why do we  need nitrogen in the first place well we  mentioned DNA earlier in some of the  other cycles but nitrogen is one of the  building blocks of DNA we look at this  picture of a nucleotide that we saw  earlier and again DNA is made up of four  nitrogen bases atcg adenine thymine  cytosine guanine they all have nitrogen  in them so the fact that DNA is is made  from nitrogen that's pretty good reason  why we need it let's go ahead and get  into the cycle so when we look at the  nitrogen cycle again the problem we  mentioned is is that the nitrogen in the  atmosphere is not usable but there are  bacteria in the soil and in the  animation you can see n2 is coming from  the atmosphere into those bacteria and  the bacteria are releasing a form of  waste called nitrates this is a more  usable form of nitrogen so this  conversion process where bacteria  convert atmospheric nitrogen into  nitrates is known as nitrogen fixation  so this is really the first key step in  the nitrogen cycle in area 2 of the  picture there are other bacteria that  will use those nitrates they'll take in  the nitrates and they'll give off and to  back into the air and you notice we've  already got ourselves a little bit of a  cycle here and we're only looking we've  only so far looked at the bacteria but  let's move on into the producers in area  3 we have our plant so the producer the  plant in this picture has roots and the  roots of course go deep into the soil  and the roots will absorb in this  KACE nitrates the nitrates that were  made by bacteria in area one the  nitrates that were made through nitrogen  fixation are taken up through the roots  and once the nitrates the nitrogen is in  the roots of a plant the nitrogen simply  moves up the food chain in area four we  have our rodents our squirrel and simply  nitrogen is moving up the food chain  you know the squirrels eating the plant  may be a snake eats the squirrel maybe  an owl or a hawk eats the snake and so  nitrogen simply moves up the food chain  and then you notice area five we have a  mushroom symbolic of our decomposers  well they feed on dead organisms so  there's nitrogen going from the squirrel  let's say the squirrel dies and it's  dead remains are fed on by the the  mushroom the decomposes notice that  there's also nitrogen coming out of  there's nitrogen coming out of the  mushroom there's nitrates coming out of  the mushroom because that's one of their  waste products so they too will I will  add nitrates back into the soil the  nitrates can then be retaken up by  plants and so notice how we have a nice  little cycle here this is known as the  nitrogen cycle I wanted to quickly  mention the role that lightning plays in  the nitrogen cycle energy from lightning  is gonna break apart the n2 and break  apart the o2 so watching my animation  here's some n2 and some oxygen that's in  the atmosphere and when it when  lightning strikes energy will break  apart those n twos and those Oh 2 's  into single ends and single O's single  nitrogen's and single oxygens when that  happens molecule called nitrogen oxide  will form and so nitrogen oxide you can  see is just form and o2 is nitrogen  oxide it's just formed and nitrogen  oxides are gonna react with rainwater  because you know with lightning usually  comes rain and so when it rains those  nitrogen oxide  aides are gonna react with rainwater to  form  no.3 nitrates and the nitrates simply  fall down to thou in the rain to the  ground and once the nitrates are in the  soil they can be absorbed through the  roots of plants like we saw a few  moments ago so I just wanted to quickly  mention the role that lightning plays in  the nitrogen cycle so there you go  there's five biogeochemical cycles you  know if you think I went a little quick  in either the water cycle the carbon  cycle the nitrogen cycle or the  phosphorous cycle I have a specific  video for each of those four you may  want to try watching those because I do  go a little slower and take my time a  little more in those specific videos so  if you're in my class pause the video  perhaps try to answer these questions on  a separate sheet of paper I'd be happy  to check your answers either before  school or after school good luck
 

